'' =============================================================================
''  test-F272 -- which %TT does a STREAMER-FED DAC need?
'' =============================================================================
''  NOT GATING.  F-272 is already RESOLVED from documentary sources
''  (P2KB-CORRECTION-FINDINGS.md:521), and the Streamer Guide ships the
''  documentary answer.  This rig exists because F-264 proved this exact axis
''  INVERTS: in DAC_MODE with the smart pin off, TT=%01 does not "enable
''  output" -- it switches the DAC's SOURCE.  So the constant that is mandatory
''  in one arrangement kills the output in the other, and both forms compile
''  clean.  A compiler cannot separate them; a jumper can.
''
''  WHAT IS AND IS NOT ALREADY MEASURED
''    EF-054 and EF-055 (2026-08-14) swept %TT on a jumpered cog-DAC pin and on
''    a level-driven one.  BOTH are graded [M-pre -- streamer-free]: they tested
''    the PIN arrangement with the streamer uninvolved.  F-272 asks the
''    streamer-fed question, which is the one arm nobody has run.
''
''  RIG: ONE JUMPER, P2 pin to P2 pin.  No external hardware.
''    DAC_PIN 0 -> ADC_PIN 1.  Pin 0's low two bits are %00, so it carries DAC
''    channel 0 (see the Streamer Guide 11.2).  Both pins are in the same 8-pin
''    power group, so they share VIO/GIO.
''
''  Build:  pnut-ts -d test-f272-streamer-dac-tt.spin2
''          The -d is REQUIRED.  Without it the debug() contents are ignored
''          and every line below prints nothing.
''
''  THE ARMS RUN IN THIS ORDER AND THE ORDER IS THE POINT
''    J   CONTINUITY, DIGITAL.  Certifies the P0-P1 short before anything
''        analog runs -- the same step EF-054 took ("continuity verified
''        digitally first") and VO-J-001 bracketed its sweep with.
''        Both legs, and the OPEN CASE HAS A DEFINED ANSWER, not a float:
''        the far pin holds the net through a 15k drive while the near pin
''        drives it hard (~30 ohm).  Shorted, the hard drive wins.  Open, the
''        15k wins and the reading INVERTS.  One leg alone cannot tell a short
''        from a stuck pin; two opposite legs can.
''    D1  DIGITAL control.  No streamer: OUT drives DAC_PIN, pinread reads it
''        back.  Must pass, or nothing digital below it is interpretable.
''    D2  the streamer's PIN path with DIR LOW -- what guide 5.1 says works.
''    D3  the same command with DIRH set first.  D2 vs D3 is an A/B on the
''        guide's claim; a D3-only pass is a MANUAL FINDING, not a rig note.
''        D1-D3 are REPORTED, NOT GATES -- see the note above arm D1.  They
''        exercise the streamer's PIN path; T0/T1 exercise its DAC path.
''    C   control, ANALOG.  Level-driven DAC exactly as the Parallax
''        documentation's own worked program does it: TT=%00, no streamer
''        routing, level written by re-issuing WRPIN with the byte in M[7:0].
''        Proves DAC + ADC across a wire J has already certified.
''    T0  streamer-fed DAC, routing X_DACS_X_X_X_0 on, TT=%00.
''    T1  the same, with P_CHANNEL (TT=%01).
''
''  READ IT LIKE THIS -- AND DOUBT THE INSTRUMENT FIRST
''    If J fails, stop and fix the bench: the program names which failure it
''    is -- no jumper, or a net something else is holding.  If D1 fails, the
''    pin cannot drive and read itself and nothing else is worth reading.
''    D2/D3 failing does NOT stop the run: the DAC path they do not touch is
''    what the experiment actually needs.  If C fails, THE RIG IS WRONG, NOT
''    THE SILICON -- and because J already proved the wire, it is NOT the
''    jumper: suspect DAC_PIN's low two bits against the channel being driven,
''    or an ADC gain that does not suit a directly-wired signal (a high-gain
''    constant saturates and reads a constant).  Do NOT interpret T0/T1
''    against a control that never responded.
''
''    EXPECTED: exactly ONE of T0/T1 shows a spread and the other sits flat.
''    That identifies the required %TT.
''
''    If BOTH track, or NEITHER does, that is a real finding and F-272's
''    premise needs revisiting.  Record it; do not force a conclusion.
''
''  WHERE THE RESULT GOES
''    An EF entry in P2-EMPIRICAL-FINDINGS.md, the test replicated to
''    engineering/ingestion/external-sources/hardware-verification/, and then
''    F-272's status updated.  Empirical outranks documentary -- but a rig that
''    never produced a control reading is not empirical.
'' =============================================================================

CON
  _xtlfreq   = 20_000_000                ' P2 Eval/Edge default -- adjust for your board
  _clkfreq   = 200_000_000

  '' EF-057: the debug interrupt corrupts streamer measurements, and the
  '' default mask puts it in ALL EIGHT cogs.  Report from cog 0 only.
  DEBUG_COGS = %0000_0001

  DAC_PIN    = 0                         ' low two bits %00 -> DAC channel 0
  ADC_PIN    = 1                         ' same 8-pin power group

  HI         = $FF                       ' full scale
  LO         = $00                       ' zero scale

  '' A spread this small is indistinguishable from no drive.  Sizing it:
  '' EF-054's readings were 1,408 for a dead pin and 6,737 for a driven one --
  '' those are ABSOLUTE single readings, not HI-vs-LO spreads, so they do not
  '' set this constant directly.  What they establish is the SCALE: a live DAC
  '' swung full range moves the ADC by something on the order of 5,000 counts.
  '' 500 sits an order of magnitude below that signal and far above the
  '' run-to-run noise of two readings taken the same way seconds apart.
  MIN_SPREAD = 500

  '' Streamer NCO at 1/1024 of sysclk.  One perpetual command then lasts far
  '' longer than the settle-plus-sample below, so the DAC is still being fed
  '' when the reading is taken.
  NCO_SLOW   = $8000_0000 / 1024

  '' X_IMM_1X32_4DAC8: one 32-bit immediate held across the four DAC bytes.
  '' Byte 0 reaches X0, and X_DACS_X_X_X_0 routes X0 to DAC channel 0.
  X_IMM_LEVEL = $7001_0000
  X_DACS_CH0  = $0400_0000               ' X_DACS_X_X_X_0
  PERPETUAL   = $FFFF                    ' 4.6: runs until stopped

  '' Arm R's command.  ONE pin (X_IMM_32X1_1DAC1, %0100) -- NOT the 32-pin
  '' X_IMM_1X32_4DAC8 the analog arms use.  See arm R's header note: with
  '' X_PINS_ON that mode drives P0..P31 and fights ADC_PIN through the jumper.
  '' Pin base is D[22:17]; count is D[15:0].  All fields disjoint, so `|`
  '' cannot carry the way `+` did in EF-054.
  R_PIN_CMD   = X_IMM_32X1_1DAC1 | X_PINS_ON | (DAC_PIN << 17) | PERPETUAL

  '' 32 identical one-bit sub-values, so the pin sits at the level from the
  '' first rollover rather than relying on the last-value-repeats rule (5.2).
  R_ALL_ONES  = $FFFF_FFFF
  R_ALL_ZEROS = $0000_0000

PUB main() | d1, d2, d3, c, t0, t1
  debug("=== F-272: which %TT does a streamer-fed DAC need? ===")

  if not arm_jumper()
    debug("STOP -- the wire is not certified. Nothing below would mean anything.")
    repeat

  '' D -- the digital ladder.  REPORTED, NOT A GATE.  T0/T1 exercise the
  '' streamer's DAC path, which is a different path from its PIN path, so a
  '' digital failure does not invalidate them.  Gating on it was a design
  '' error: it stopped two bench runs before the actual experiment.
  d1 := arm_plain_drive()
  debug("D1 plain pin drive, no streamer        : ", udec_(d1), " of 8")
  if d1 <> 8
    debug("D1 RED -- the pin cannot drive and read itself back. STOP.")
    repeat

  d2 := arm_stream_digital(false)
  d3 := arm_stream_digital(true)
  debug("D2 streamer -> pin, DIR LOW  (5.1 says): ", udec_(d2), " of 8")
  debug("D3 streamer -> pin, DIR HIGH (dirh set): ", udec_(d3), " of 8")
  report_digital(d2, d3)

  c := arm_level_dac()
  debug("C  level DAC, TT=%00, no routing       : spread=", udec_(c))
  if c < MIN_SPREAD
    debug("C RED -- the RIG is wrong, not the silicon -- but J proved the wire,")
    debug("         so it is NOT the jumper. Suspect the DAC channel mapping or")
    debug("         the ADC gain. See the header. STOP.")
    repeat

  t0 := arm_streamer_dac(false)
  t1 := arm_streamer_dac(true)
  debug("T0 streamer-fed DAC, TT=%00            : spread=", udec_(t0))
  debug("T1 streamer-fed DAC, TT=%01 P_CHANNEL  : spread=", udec_(t1))

  verdict(t0, t1)
  debug("=== complete ===")
  repeat


PRI verdict(t0, t1)
  if t0 >= MIN_SPREAD and t1 < MIN_SPREAD
    debug("VERDICT: a streamer-fed DAC needs TT=%00 -- P_CHANNEL kills it.")
  elseif t1 >= MIN_SPREAD and t0 < MIN_SPREAD
    debug("VERDICT: a streamer-fed DAC needs TT=%01 (P_CHANNEL). Matches F-272.")
  elseif t0 >= MIN_SPREAD and t1 >= MIN_SPREAD
    debug("VERDICT: BOTH drove. F-272's premise needs revisiting -- record it.")
  else
    debug("VERDICT: NEITHER drove, yet C did. Real finding -- do not force it.")


'' --- J: is the jumper actually there? ---------------------------------------
'' Certifies the short BEFORE anything analog, with BOTH legs, and with a
'' DEFINED open-circuit answer rather than a float.  ADC_PIN holds the net
'' through a 15k drive; DAC_PIN drives it hard.  Shorted, the hard drive wins.
'' Open, the 15k wins and the reading inverts.  A single leg cannot separate
'' "shorted" from "stuck at that level" -- two opposite legs can.
PRI arm_jumper() : ok | pull_lo, pull_hi, short_hi, short_lo
  '' J0 -- certify the instrument before the wire.  With DAC_PIN released, the
  '' 15k drive alone must own the net.  If it does not, something else is
  '' holding ADC_PIN and J1/J2 would be reading that, not the jumper.
  reset_pins()
  pull_lo := weak_read(P_LOW_15K, 0)              ' expect 0
  pull_hi := weak_read(P_HIGH_15K, 1)             ' expect 1
  debug("J0 15k pull alone, DAC_PIN released    : ", udec_(pull_lo), " / ", udec_(pull_hi), "   (want 0 / 1)")
  if pull_lo <> 0 or pull_hi <> 1
    debug("J0 RED -- the 15k drive does not own the net; something else holds it. STOP.")
    return false

  '' J1 / J2 -- now fight that weak drive with a hard one across the wire.
  short_hi := drive_vs_pull(true, P_LOW_15K, 0)   ' hard HIGH vs 15k low  -> 1 shorted
  short_lo := drive_vs_pull(false, P_HIGH_15K, 1) ' hard LOW  vs 15k high -> 0 shorted
  debug("J1 DAC_PIN hard HIGH vs 15k pull-down  : ", udec_(short_hi), "   (1=shorted 0=open)")
  debug("J2 DAC_PIN hard LOW  vs 15k pull-up    : ", udec_(short_lo), "   (0=shorted 1=open)")
  reset_pins()

  if short_hi == 1 and short_lo == 0
    debug("J  CONTINUITY CERTIFIED -- both legs agree the pins are shorted.")
    return true
  if short_hi == 0 and short_lo == 1
    debug("J RED -- THE JUMPER IS NOT CONNECTED. Both legs read the OPEN value.")
    debug("         Fit a wire P", udec_(DAC_PIN), " to P", udec_(ADC_PIN), " and run again.")
    return false
  debug("J RED -- the legs DISAGREE: a pin is stuck, or the net is held elsewhere.")
  return false


'' ADC_PIN drives its own level through 15k, then reads the pad back.  Whoever
'' wins the net -- this weak drive or something stronger on the wire -- is what
'' pinread() returns.
PRI weak_read(pull_mode, out_state) : level
  pinclear(ADC_PIN)
  wrpin(ADC_PIN, pull_mode)
  if out_state
    pinhigh(ADC_PIN)                              ' DIR=1 OUT=1 -> 15k to VIO
  else
    pinlow(ADC_PIN)                               ' DIR=1 OUT=0 -> 15k to GIO
  waitms(1)                                       ' 15k into pin+wire C: sub-us
  level := pinread(ADC_PIN)


PRI drive_vs_pull(drive_high, pull_mode, out_state) : level
  reset_pins()
  pinclear(DAC_PIN)                               ' mode 0 -> normal hard drive
  if drive_high
    pinhigh(DAC_PIN)
  else
    pinlow(DAC_PIN)
  level := weak_read(pull_mode, out_state)


'' --- D: does the streamer's PIN path work in this cog at all? ---------------
'' A LADDER, simplest layer first, and NONE of it gates the experiment.
''
'' TWO BENCH RUNS, TWO WRONG ANSWERS FROM ME -- read this before theorising.
'' Run 1 (13:53) scored 4 of 8.  I explained it: the arm had reused
'' X_IMM_1X32_4DAC8, which is a 32-PIN mode (guide 5.2 and Appendix A both say
'' so), so X_PINS_ON drove P0..P31, held ADC_PIN low against DAC_PIN's high,
'' and the jumper made that a drive fight.  That was a real bug and the one-pin
'' X_IMM_32X1_1DAC1 is the right fix.  IT WAS NOT THE CAUSE.  Run 2 (14:00) ran
'' the one-pin mode and scored 3 of 8.
''
'' The lesson is in the arithmetic.  4 of 8 is what a CONSTANT readback scores
'' against an alternating want -- and it is also the expected score of a pin
'' reading NOTHING IN PARTICULAR.  I read a specific mechanism into a number
'' that a coin flip produces just as readily, and run 2's 3 of 8 is what an
'' undriven pin looks like when the charge on it happens to decay.  One number,
'' two mechanisms, and I picked the interesting one.
''
'' So this ladder stops guessing and separates the layers:
''   D1  no streamer at all -- OUT drives the pin, pinread reads it back.
''       The control.  Must pass, or nothing below is interpretable.
''   D2  streamer -> pin with DIR LOW.  This is what the guide's 11.0 callout
''       says works: "Ordinary pin output through X_PINS_ON ... requires no
''       WRPIN and no DIRH."  Two runs of this scored 4 and 3.
''   D3  the same command with DIRH set first.  If D3 passes where D2 fails,
''       the guide's claim is WRONG ON SILICON and that is a manual finding,
''       not a rig note.
''
'' PREDICTION ON RECORD, SO THE BENCH CAN FALSIFY IT: D3 passes, D2 fails.
'' Filed as F-308 from the Silicon Doc v35 itself, which says the streamer's
'' pin data is OR'd "with {OUTB, OUTA} to produce the final 64 pin OUTPUT
'' STATES", while separately "an I/O pin's output ENABLE is controlled by its
'' DIR bit".  Streamer -> OUT; DIR still gates the driver.  Nothing gives the
'' streamer authority over DIR -- only a smart pin's %TT does that, and this
'' path uses no smart pin.  IF D2 PASSES, F-308 IS WRONG AND GETS REVERSED.
''
'' D1 -- the control for the whole ladder.  No streamer anywhere: the pin is
'' driven by OUT and read straight back.  If this cannot manage 8 of 8 then
'' nothing about pins on this board is trustworthy and D2/D3 mean nothing.
PRI arm_plain_drive() : hits | i, want
  repeat i from 0 to 7
    want := i & 1
    reset_pins()
    pinclear(DAC_PIN)
    if want
      pinhigh(DAC_PIN)
    else
      pinlow(DAC_PIN)
    waitms(1)
    if pinread(DAC_PIN) == want
      hits++
  reset_pins()


'' D2 / D3 -- the same streamer command, run twice, differing ONLY in whether
'' the pin was enabled first.  D3 starts with OUT LOW, so if the pin follows
'' the stream the streamer must have overridden OUT and the result is
'' unambiguous.  This is the A/B that settles the guide's 5.1 claim.
PRI arm_stream_digital(set_dir) : hits | i, want
  repeat i from 0 to 7
    want := i & 1
    reset_pins()
    pinclear(DAC_PIN)
    if set_dir
      pinlow(DAC_PIN)                  ' DIR=1, OUT=0 -- streamer must override
    stream_start(R_PIN_CMD, want ? R_ALL_ONES : R_ALL_ZEROS)
    waitms(2)
    if pinread(DAC_PIN) == want
      hits++
  stream_stop()
  reset_pins()


PRI report_digital(d2, d3)
  if d2 == 8 and d3 <> 8
    debug("D  Streamer pin output works with DIR LOW. REVERSE F-308 -- the")
    debug("   guide's 11.0 callout is right and the Silicon Doc read was not.")
  elseif d3 == 8 and d2 <> 8
    debug("D  F-308 CONFIRMED ON SILICON: streamer pin output NEEDS DIRH.")
    debug("   Fix streamer-body.md:834 and the F-305 note that repeats it.")
  elseif d2 == 8 and d3 == 8
    debug("D  Both drove; DIR is not the variable. Guide 5.1 stands.")
  else
    debug("D  NEITHER drove the pin, yet D1 did. The streamer's PIN path is")
    debug("   not running in this cog. Prime suspect is EF-057: the debug")
    debug("   interrupt is live in cog 0 and this rig MEASURES in cog 0 --")
    debug("   the EF-057 fix launches the measurement in another cog.")
    debug("   NOT fatal here: T0/T1 use the DAC path, and C validates that.")


'' --- C: the documentation's own level-driven DAC ----------------------------
'' TT=%00, so the DAC takes its value from the pin's own M[7:0] level field,
'' re-written by re-issuing WRPIN.  No streamer, no routing, no COGID.
PRI arm_level_dac() : spread
  spread := sense(level_dac(HI), level_dac(LO))


PRI level_dac(level) : counts
  reset_pins()
  pinclear(DAC_PIN)
  wrpin(DAC_PIN, P_DAC_124R_3V | (level << 8))   ' level -> M[7:0]
  pinlow(DAC_PIN)                                 ' DIRH; pin drives
  counts := read_adc()


'' --- T0 / T1: the streamer feeds the DAC ------------------------------------
'' The routed channel is this cog's, so M[3:0] carries this cog's ID (11.0).
PRI arm_streamer_dac(use_channel) : spread
  spread := sense(streamer_dac(HI, use_channel), streamer_dac(LO, use_channel))


PRI streamer_dac(level, use_channel) : counts | mode
  reset_pins()
  mode := P_DAC_124R_3V | (cogid() << 8)          ' COGID -> M[3:0]
  if use_channel
    mode |= P_CHANNEL                             ' TT=%01
  pinclear(DAC_PIN)
  wrpin(DAC_PIN, mode)
  pinlow(DAC_PIN)                                 ' DIRH; pin drives
  stream_start(X_IMM_LEVEL | X_DACS_CH0 | PERPETUAL, level)
  counts := read_adc()
  stream_stop()


'' --- shared measurement -----------------------------------------------------
PRI read_adc() : counts
  pinclear(ADC_PIN)
  wrpin(ADC_PIN, P_ADC_1X | P_ADC)      ' unity gain: a directly-wired signal
  wxpin(ADC_PIN, 12)                    ' 2^12-clock sample period
  pinhigh(ADC_PIN)
  waitms(5)                             ' settle, then one full period
  counts := rdpin(ADC_PIN) & $7FFF_FFFF ' C rides in bit 31; mask it off


PRI sense(a, b) : spread
  spread := a > b ? a - b : b - a


PRI reset_pins()
  stream_stop()
  pinclear(DAC_PIN)
  pinclear(ADC_PIN)


PRI stream_start(cmd, sval)
  org
    setxfrq ##NCO_SLOW
    xinit   cmd, sval
  end


PRI stream_stop()
  org
    xstop
  end
